Meanwhile, the worlds begin to overlay.

On Ravnica, high above Tin Street, a troupe of Rakdos fools steps outside for their break. They look up and see a second Ravnica floating above the first, a ghostly mirror lined up perfectly, roof by roof, spire by spire. They scramble to the center of the road, pointing and screaming—largely ignored at first, as pointing and screaming is the way of Rakdos fools—until the banker driving his thrull pulls the reins to stop and sees his own doppelgänger upside down in the sky above.

And on Bloomburrow, great trees curl alongside existing ones, strange doppelgängers scurrying from bottom to top. Squirrelfolk chitter downward with grim warnings and dire reports. A world is appearing alongside theirs, shifted slightly to the west by a paw's length. The illusion is harder to see, but when Mabel squints her eyes, she can make out a shadow of a tree, of her home, of her littles.

Amonkhet is the most alarmed. Snow-capped pyramids float above Naktamun, ready to descend. The air feels cooler. Bright day feels balmy as night. Samut shivers, looking up to Hazoret beside her for guidance. The god nods at her, at Djeru, and the three of them venture into the chill of daytime to face the illusion high above.

And simultaneously, on Dominaria, Danitha Capashen sees mirrored, on the wide plain before her, a specter of an army, vast and imposing, the hint of a bog beneath them and a great many-toothed beast at its center. She unsheathes her sword and calls to the host of angels above to ready for an attack. But she holds, uncertain, watching as the center moves, waiting for its form to become corporeal.

