The world twins overhead, and as Tam bolts out of the tower, she can almost _ feel _ this world combining with its double. The true Strixhaven hangs above as a canopy, and all around her it merges into fullness.

She's too late. Jadzi must be dead.

_ It's my fault, it's my fault, it's my fault … _

Part of her senses, _ knows _, that Hexhaven merges first in the overlay. It's woven into the spell. She can feel it in her fingertips. The people will merge last, but first they all must spill into the same place. Sure enough, as she runs, she starts to see more appearing—Strixhaven students emerging as ghosts that solidify into corporeality, entire buildings shoving in alongside existing ones, even birds and creatures spilling over as spirits and flying off as matter. Hexhaven and Strixhaven are becoming one.

The alternate and original curl together. Half the tower's exterior takes on an entirely different stone, while errant Strixhaven students appear, then wander, frightened and confused. The blank-eyed doppelgängers of Hexhaven flock to these new students, to the familiar features of the true Strixhaven, and attack without hesitation. As Tam bolts for cover, she sees them corral a professor she recognizes from her time at the university. Dellian Fel does not run, because he does not need to run. His black robes billow as he walks with cold purpose, conjuring vile-looking plants with each step, vines with broad leaves trailing high overhead, shielding himself from falling rubble with a flick of his fingers. His ever-present sneer remains on his lips, as if the merging of worlds were a mere annoyance rather than a catastrophe. Tam keeps running. Professor Fel can handle himself.

Rubble falls, pieces of Strixhaven dislodging as Hexhaven overtakes its form. She hears screams, senses terror projected from the minds around her. Tam feels what they feel. The barrier between her mind and theirs is as thin as the one between worlds. She tries to lock it out, shove it away.

"827," she says aloud as she runs. "829. 839."

It's calming enough. The voices in her mind vanish, replaced by the voices in her ears. Tam grits her teeth.

But what can she do about the overlay? She runs, and thinks. The Theorist taught her everything he knew, all of it except for the one final observation he saved for himself. He knows how two planes combined … but not how to separate them once the process begins. Surely there's part of her study she could summon. Surely this Multiverse will hear her blood sing the same song as her maker. Perhaps it can see her as its god, too.

Tam pauses in the throng of cadets and students, blank-eyed Hexhaven and terrified Strixhaven alike, and reaches with her mind. She feels for the edges of the Echoverse high above and can sense its seam. She grimaces, and tries to push, to no avail. A thousand threads of connection are woven between this world and that. The sky is filled with falling debris and panicking sphinxes. It is hard to tell which parts belong to this world and which belong to the original. A handful of Konstrari cadets attempt to move the pieces midair, nudging them to fall as keystones in arches or alongside existing buildings.

#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/02.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

Somewhere in the screams of the crowd, she hears a single voice. It stops her in her tracks. The crowd moves around her as a river around a rock.

_ Kirol. _

They're yelling, somewhere, and as Tam looks up, she can see pieces of rubble falling on the throng of moving people. She instinctively runs toward their voice. But as she does, she remembers.

_ I betrayed them. _ Her heart races. _ I betrayed all of my friends. _ She grits her teeth in self-hatred and shakes her head. _ Screw it. They can hate me, so long as they're alive. _

Tam sees them, spots the brawny vampire from a distance, sees the cobblestones of another world falling toward them from the sky. She reaches out, angles and lines of light a cage around her hand, and _ pulls _ at the threads of chance that crisscross high above.

A flying sphinx suddenly swerves to the right, wings wide, and in a moment of forgetting to glance up is pummeled by the clatter of cobblestones raining from the sky. It cries out, a catlike howl, and careens to the earth a distance away. Tam negates her spell and looks for Kirol. They stare back at her, eyes wide, body frozen in shock. Their expression is overcome with emotion. This is someone happy to the point of tears to see that she is alive. This is a friend.

"Kirol!"

Kirol lurches forward, shoving past the throngs of people between them, and envelops Tam in a hug.

Tam clings back, shocked, desperate, hungry for the care of her friend. "Are you hurt?"

They shake their head. "No. How are you? You're here. You betrayed us. You kidnapped … I hate y—" They squeeze her with anything but hate. "How could you do this to us?" Their breath shudders. "I want _ answers _, Tam."

She buries her face in their chest. "I'm sorry, Kirol. I'm sorry. I was answering to someone I couldn't say no to. Jadzi is dead. It's my fault." She wants to cry, but there isn't enough in her to get there. She grips tight, unwilling to be parted from her friend again.

The noise above is getting worse. More of Strixhaven appears around them.

Kirol looks down at Tam. "I don't trust you in the slightest, but I don't want you dead."

"I don't want you dead, either."

"Cool."

"Cool."

Resolution achieved, the vampire blurts, "Tam, what in two halves is happening?"

She startles at hearing Arcavian slang for the first time in months. Right. Task at hand. "Two Multiverses, one is a copy. The copy is overlaying the original. We're watching them combine."

Kirol steps back. "No, Tam. You. What is happening?"

_ Why did you betray our trust? _ is left unsaid. Tam feels like half a person still. She wishes she had courage, so she pretends it's there and answers truthfully. "You trusted me, and I failed you. So it's my job to fix it."

Kirol doesn't say anything. Their face is unreadable. Familiar and unfamiliar at once.

"What am I supposed to do?" they ask in monotone. "Hide while you fix it?"

"I don't want you getting hurt."

They don't seem to buy that.

Tam's explanation tumbles out. "My teacher didn't make a way to undo it! He created a theorem that can't be reversed. It just keeps going as long as part of him is alive." Her chest hurts. She grimaces. "I have to find a way because it's on _ me _, Kirol. Every living thing in the Multiverse dies if I can't find a way to stop the overlay."

Kirol is breathing deeply. A harsh line is carved between their eyebrows, and their chest rises and falls. They close their eyes, then open them with sudden clarity.

"You're going to solve it," they say with certainty.

Tam shakes her head. "How?"

"I don't know. But you're the smartest person I know. Use your _ whole _ head."

"Kirol—"

"I'm gonna find the others. This is Strixhaven-but-not-Strixhaven, yeah? There's a Biblioplex?"

"It's a Folioplex," she corrects, near tears.

They make a face. "_ Folio- _plex?"

"I _ know _," Tam says apologetically as the tears arrive.

They put a hand on her shoulder. "Find me when you have the answer. I'm going to look for our friends."

_ Our friends. _

"Hold in your mind where you want to go," Tam says. "My maker's telepathy is a part of this plane. It'll lead you there."

Kirol groans.

"I know!" Tam groans back.

They bolt, and Tam watches. Her chest feels tight. Did they forgive her? Was her act of saving them enough? _ They're alive, and so are our friends _, she reminds herself. _ That's what counts. _

One world appears on another.

Trees, now, are surfacing and solidifying. Dragons, too, high above and far away. More students arrive, and what was already a crowd begins to thicken into something more chaotic and dangerous. As Tam runs through them, they crush around her, and with effort, she makes her way to a pocket of calm to catch her breath. They're running, but no one knows where to. Screaming, but no one knows why. The volume of people in danger becomes clear.

_ And this is happening all over the Multiverse. Every plane, every world, looking at its double. _

Tam feels small. She's only one mage. She's not a peerless telepath. She's an apprentice, a student.

_ I'm not even an entire person. _ She is unfinished, incomplete, the creativity and cleverness of one of the greatest mages in all the Multiverse and nothing more. In this moment, Tam feels that she _ is _ inadequacy. She is swallowed once more by the crowd, and she tumbles into the throng lost and afraid.

