#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/03.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

The final memory room is not a trap but a reunion.

The mage-ring spits Vraska out in the center of what she knows—only from stories—is the center of the Implicit Maze. Banners of the ten guilds flutter high above, and she stands in a broad forum with an immense floating monolith at its center. Mana pulses within, and there are stone pillars around its perimeter, one for each guild. It's clear a fight has taken place. A massive suit of armor has been blown to pieces, its sunburst shield on the ground, its helmet lodged in the wall.

"Vraska!"

She flinches and turns—and is met with a full-body tackle-hug from Chandra. "You jumped off a building!"

"I did. I landed in a portal. I got the last three parts. Soldier, Guildpact, Survivor. What about you?"

Chandra grimaces. "We tried to face him. Got pretty far, but we weren't enough to stop him. He had our doubles tackle us through portals, which sent us here. Now we've been dealing with _ you _."

_ Me? _ It takes a moment for Vraska to realize who she's talking about. "The other me," she groans. "I can't stop a moving statue."

"Well, you need to _ try _ so we can get back to the _ other _ versions of us, because they're blocking the one mage-ring out of here."

Vraska tries to sort out where _ here _ is. This is the Implicit Maze, the moment Jace became the Living Guildpact. He hasn't told her much about that moment, only that the rest of the runners were about to kill each other. Is that what they'll have to do here to escape?

"Where's the other Vraska?" she asks.

The answer is a loud boom that shakes the ground. Vraska follows Chandra's wide, panicked stare and finds the source.

The other Vraska sits atop the head of a stone Niv-Mizzet. It towers over the (already tall) walls of the maze—once a statue, now the living will of the gorgon at its crown. The dragon's wings spread with the rumble of tumbling gravel; its claws scrape the ground like pickaxes on a mountainside. The dragon bellows, and the sound is a landslide. It seems preoccupied with something at its feet. Vraska is relieved that it doesn't seem to take notice of her arrival.

"Where are the others?" she asks.

"Near the tail. Garruk's trying to get up top."

"No other doubles?"

"I told you, they're guarding the mage-ring in the back."

"And it's just the dragon?"

Chandra's face contorts between exaggerated anger and exasperation as she snaps, "Yeah, it's _ just _ the dragon!"

Vraska runs for the nearby rubble and drags the suit of armor's shield out with great effort. Chandra does nothing for a moment, then joins her.

"Closer," Vraska instructs. "Make sure it doesn't see us."

"What is your plan, exactly?"

"You're going to launch me high enough that I can petrify my double."

Whatever negative feelings have been roiling in Chandra vanish as soon as Vraska says _ launch _. Her face erupts in a ferocious grin. "Oh, I know this trick! Just tell me when to fire."

#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/04.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

The dragon lumbers forward, and from their side of the wall within the maze they can tell it hasn't taken notice of their machinations. Vraska hears Garruk and Ajani, the clash of their axes, the laugh of the other Vraska high above.

Vraska realizes she doesn't know how to aim whatever Chandra is about to do. _ Help? _ she asks the geniuses in her brain.

_ Turn the shield toward the target _, says the Guildpact.

_ Tell Chandra to aim it at forty-seven degrees _, says the Detective.

_ Don't flinch _, says the Nihilist. _ Fly like there's no tomorrow. _

_ Tuck and roll _, adds the Survivor.

_ And kill swiftly _, finishes the Soldier.

"Forty-seven degrees!" Vraska yells.

The two of them hoist the shield on top of a piece of rubble, and Chandra eyeballs the sides. "Good!"

Vraska climbs on top. "On my mark!"

She watches the statue, sees its head turn to face them. The gorgon on top flinches, realizing who's below.

"Now!" Vraska calls.

Chandra's explosion takes her breath away, and the propulsion beneath her feet vibrates through her shinbones. She is launched, and the wind—the wind! It hisses past her head, and her breath catches in her chest as she is thrust high into the air.

#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/05.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

The dragon turns below her, and Vraska tucks her head to roll on impact.

She feels her spine bruise instantly as she hits the rock, but it's better than broken legs. She ducks around the stone frill of the dragon's head and looks down toward its tail.

"Hello, me!" the other Vraska calls. She laughs, and her gown ripples in the breeze as the dragon statue takes another step forward.

But the true Vraska isn't watching her doppelgänger. Far below her are the other doubles, guarding the mage-ring and looking around to see what caused the explosion. The dragon's tail lifts and lands between them and the mage-ring.

Vraska bursts from behind the frill and looks her double straight in the eyes.

This other Vraska has never known pain. She is proud and flawless. "Why not make this fun?" she says. "Let's fight as assassins do. Knives on knives." She pulls out her own blade for emphasis.

Vraska doesn't consider it for a second, because when she locks eyes with her other self, all she sees is a pair of lovely blue irises, dull and mundane, not a flicker of gorgon magic within them. She's already won. She's been charging her gaze since she launched into the air. She grabs her double by the shoulders, holding her in place. Skin turns to stone, and the other Vraska solidifies with a look of mild confusion on her face. The dragon freezes, its spell broken, and Vraska shoves the gorgon statue over the side of its stone snout.

"Now!" she yells, as the other Vraska hits the ground in a spray of broken rock.

Below, her friends trade targets.

Garruk bounds over the dragon statue's tail, nimbly traversing the ice field the cryomancer Chandra summons, knocking her to the ground with a swing of his axe. Ajani picks his way through every beast the Chain-Veiled Garruk summons, roaring as he rips the ancient artifact away with a swipe of his claws. And Chandra howls a war cry as she sends a wave of fire toward the battle-scarred Ajani, overwhelming Vraska's senses with the stench of burning fur.

"Leave them!" Vraska cries, skidding down the back of the dragon statue, and they abandon their distracted counterparts to join her.

She looks around for her petrified counterpart and finds her nearby, her body shattered but her dress intact. "I've got a plan," she says to Chandra. "Help me get her dress off."

Chandra blushes.

"For a disguise, lady-killer."

"Oh."

After some shimmying, Vraska raises the hood over her head. With a nod, she leads the others through the mage-ring to their final stop.

For the first time in years, she feels proud.

