The Theorist is hard at work.

He understands how it all fits together, a single gargantuan machine with infinite parts. He feels for the edges of both Multiverses, omniscience still ringing through his veins, and reaches his gift through space and time to seize them both, weaving them together with threads of himself. They bind with ease, a fiber poked with a needle into felted wool, but he's losing himself, thread by thread. There's so little left.

The Theorist grits his teeth and concentrates, continues the overlay. A wad of consciousness is stuck to his handiwork. He swears aloud, pieces it apart from the sides of each Multiverse, and recognizes as he does that he's forgotten the order of the alphabet.

The Theorist swears louder. Sweat stings his eyes, and he tries to blink it away with his body while his mind once more grabs hold of the far edge of the old Multiverse. His perception is multitudinous—inside himself and outside himself and underneath what feels like time itself. Somewhere, his back hurts. Somewhere, fingertips without a body brush the tops of fourteen planes, like skimming a hand across the surface of the ocean. It is too much. It is just enough. He can wield it now that he knows the pattern. The mind sculptor laughs, and it thunders through Kaldheim. He blinks away sweat, and it rains on Innistrad. For a moment, he sees outside himself and marvels. _ I know everything. I _ am _ everything. I have reached the apex of ability. Beyond sphinx and dragon, I stand at the end of the Multiverses. _

He summons the mage-rings in his room and arranges them to the side. With a flicker of blue light and not an ounce of effort, the Theorist reaches through the tower and commands his doppelgängers, his soldiers, _ Kill anyone who attempts to stop the overlay. _ The repercussions don't matter. He is everything. He sees all.

The Theorist aligns his mage-rings, stand-ins, dummies, maquettes of his alternate planes, in the order he now knows is correct. But something isn't perfect. Despite his omniscience, his body is falling apart. He's a mess, bloody nose and red eyes. He presses further, and away go his memories—of extorting Ravnican bourgeoisie, his first fistfight, his first kill, his first kiss. But none of that matters in the mind of a god. He tightens his grip, and an earthquake trembles through Dominaria.

He feels taxed and invigorated, boundless and tethered at once. It nearly sends him careening back to the ground. But as he works, he senses something through the netting of space. An error. A thread out of place. He investigates, pressing his consciousness forward to see …

The aberrations are up to something.

From his all-seeing height of consciousness, the Theorist wavers.

"What a mess," says a voice near his body.

His omniscience is interrupted.

Consciousness careens through space-time back to his small, confining, _ annoying _ physical form, and he sees in front of him Liliana, Professor Opal at her side as a lich.

The Theorist sneers and raises a hand to snap them both to sleep. "I'm busy."

"I know what you're feeling right now, Jace."

He nearly snarls back that he doesn't have time for her, but she keeps going (she always kept going).

"This doesn't have to be how your story ends."

The Theorist glances upward to return to omniscience once more, then stalls. His lip curls in distaste. "Did you have a change of heart after you betrayed us for the dragon? I let you live then. I won't do it now."

"I was wrong, and I chose to change_ . _" She's serious. He's never seen her serious. "There's no Gideon here this time. Only you."

"Stop," he commands Liliana.

She does, but it's her reanimated doppelgänger that replies, "I am _ un _stoppable, and you know it."

Hairs rise on the back of his neck. He hates her. Even with so little of him left, he _ hates _ her. She was always like this, always so domineering. There's a part of him, still, that remembers how she'd grab his leash and force him to her will. It's this part that negates the command.

"Jace, I need you to do what I couldn't."

"A do-over is in your interests, too," the Theorist notes. "You could have a life where you were loyal to the rest of us."

Liliana and her doppelgänger share a pithy glance. The lich breaks first and laughs, a bubbly and lovely sound. "Loyalty's cheap. But authenticity? _ That's _ wealth."

The sanctum is vibrating with power. The Theorist still has astral hands on the edges of the two Multiverses. His eyes are faraway and infinite. "How much suffering did we put each other through? How much pain and misery could have been avoided?"

"Can it, Beleren!" yells her lich, and the original Liliana continues, "When I was in your shoes, it didn't earn me safety, or freedom, or power."

"Even now, you're a manipulative snake," the Theorist says.

Liliana ignores his insult. Instead, her eyes dart to his hands. "Why are you fidgeting?"

The Theorist tries to still his hands. Ten trillion leagues away, his consciousness momentarily pauses its busywork. "You were my partner, yet you treated me like a dog you found on the street. Why should I trust you?"

"Because I _ was _ you! I betrayed my allies and I betrayed myself and all I won was loneliness!" He can see the Chain Veil in her mind, see the woman he once adored become a god of death. Her eyes grow dark. "I know what it feels like to wield the power you do, and I know that all it brings is emptiness."

A nearby portal glows, and this Multiverse's Vraska steps through. She smiles, moving to his side without fanfare.

The Theorist opens his mouth to address Liliana, but he doesn't get a word out before the reanimated corpse of Professor Opal tackles him. He hits the floor in a daze, head cracking against the tiles.

#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/06.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

The distant self among the cosmos vanishes, but the worlds continue to weave themselves together.

All of him crashes back to earth, and before he can put Liliana to sleep, the lich kicks him in the head. He looks to Vraska for help and sees a blur of white as the lich kicks him again.

The world spins, and both Lilianas yell, "Undo it, Beleren! Come on, genius!"

"No!"

"Do it now!"

"I can't undo it!"

The lich lifts his head up and smashes it against the floor. Why isn't Vraska helping?

Enraged, he unleashes his mind.

His power hurtles toward Liliana as a wave, first pulling her consciousness out and toward him, then pushing forward with a violent psychic shove. He launches himself into her mind and, as he does, brings every torture he can think of with him. In a millisecond he scrapes up the worst memories at the bottom of her psyche—her brother sputtering and ill, Gideon's skin curdling again and again. He drowns her heart in the image of himself failing to stop Phyrexia. To top it off, he crams a scribble of a theorem between her frontal lobes and lets a tumor charge into existence.

"Bastard!" Liliana chokes out.

As she does, her double puts a hand to her head, and a surge of magic blinds the Theorist. He blinks away the pain as Liliana shakes herself off, healed. She launches at him, pummeling with her fists. But the Theorist swims through her mind still, finds the node that reminds her heart to beat, and curls himself around it with dark curiosity.

A flash of white cloth, and he realizes Vraska has at last decided to intervene.

But it isn't …

It isn't what he expects.

Something sharp pierces the Theorist's heart, and he gasps. The only mortal to have achieved omniscience failed to see it coming.

"Now," says a voice from behind him.

Chandra, Ajani, and Garruk, bruised and battle-worn, emerge from a portal in front of him. Too late, he realizes that it is his Vraska at his back, her arm wrapped around him, clutching the knife in his chest. He reaches to pull it out, but he is interrupted as every single one of his aberrations rushes into him at once.

They overtake his mind like a tsunami.

The Theorist panics, tries to contain their incorporeal forms, but they're _ him _. They're too fast, too agile. They hit harder than they should. _ Weaklings, faults, as easily carved away as the peel of an apple. Why didn't they stay out of my body like I told them to? _ The Theorist tries to build a levee to keep them at bay, but he cannot stop the strength of the sea. They bombard his mind, taking hold one by one.

The Nihilist slams a wall around his consciousness; the Theorist cannot get out. He tries to reach out telepathically for Liliana's mastery of death, for Chandra's pyromancy, for Garruk and Ajani's muscle, but he is met with lead and the weight of endless hopelessness.

The Detective snips the connection between the Theorist and the weaving of the Multiverses; he cannot continue. The Theorist scrambles and reaches for that feeling of omniscience, that tantalizing velvet of the grip he had on two entire planes, but it slips through his fingers, frayed and forgotten. The Theorist cries out, desperate to prevent it, clinging to life, to success, to victory.

The Warden forces him to kneel; he cannot move. He pulls against his own body and cramps in the process. He rages to feel his muscles controlled again, and the Warden fills his mind with visions of Alhammarret, of Tezzeret, of Elesh Norn, of all the monsters that forced him to hurt others. The Theorist feels small and helpless. He howls in fear.

Amid that terror and fury, the Clean Slate carves out a clean path to their body's brain stem; the Theorist is raw and vulnerable. He watches as the space of his mind parts, unable to stop it and painfully aware of the area exposed. He breathes and savors his breaths; he knows he has few left.

The Guildpact grabs hold of each aberration, binding them all together; he is reintegrating. It is gentle, like stepping into a warm spring. Each part slips into place with ease, warmth, as many slowly form back into one. His pragmatism, his curiosity, his guardedness, his courage, his grit, his empathy—they grab hold of the Theorist's cold logic and suddenly all are united save two.

For the Survivor stands aside, his duty unneeded, and the Soldier slips through like a trained assassin, locating the part of his body's mind that remembers how to breathe. He plucks it out.

The body gasps, and the Theorist, that cold and wounded child, is embraced by the rest—taken in, soothed, and sent down and away to rest.

Plural becomes singular. The aberrations, the parts, become one. The man opens his eyes.

He is kneeling in a tall sanctum built by the part of himself he hates the most—which, despite his feelings, is also him. An orrery spins above, and scattered around the room are mage-rings … and friends.

His name is Jace.

He feels something cold and sharp piercing his chest. He feels warm hands on his shoulders, a body against his back.

Jace blinks, feels a trickle of blood run down from his mouth, and moves his hand over Vraska's. They grip the knife together. It worked. The tether between the Multiverses is fragile. Holmberg's Paradox—the illusion is inherently connected to the wielder's physical body.

Vraska guides him down. They lie back, her legs curled around him. The Ochran dagger in his heart is a promise fulfilled. How strange it is to be complete.

"Did it work?" Vraska asks.

#figure(image("009_Episode 9- Unafraid/07.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

Jace knows it did. But before he says so, he looks at each of his rescuers, his opponents, his friends. One by one, he gifts them what he needs them to hear.

He offers one reassurance … and an apology. _ We fought like cats, but you're the little sister I never had. I'm sorry I did a bad job at showing it. _

Another gets instructions. _ The overlay is stopped, but the Multiverses are still intertwined. You need to eliminate mine. Find Tam. She'll learn the plan shortly. Thank you. You were the best of us. _

He offers another a warning. _ Thank you for protecting the most vulnerable part of me. I'm sorry I couldn't cure you. The problem you're trying to solve won't be fixed in the jungle. _

A fourth gets a fond farewell. _ Thank you for the lessons you taught without meaning to. You can feel however you like when I die. I'm fine with that. _

But that fourth says something back, silent, unheard. A feeling, rather than words. An aching grief, a genuine warmth, the notion of centuries and memory. Earnestness. Apology. It makes Jace's eyes widen in surprise.

_ Really? _ he asks, touched.

Liliana smiles. _ I'm sorry, Jace. Rest unburdened. _

He coughs out a breath. Lip trembling, he nods. _ Thank you, Lili. _

"Yes," he finally says aloud to Vraska, squeezing her hand.

She squeezes back and places her forehead against his. "Do you want to talk somewhere else?"

"No," he says aloud, voice thin. "I want it to be real. I want to look at you."

He feels … heavy. Above, the orrery has paused, the threads tying the two Multiverses together fading with the beating of his heart. He doesn't feel sad to see it fail, now. Suffering will continue, because that is the cost of being alive.

"The cost is worth it," he says, looking into his beloved's eyes. He feels the warmth of those who care for him, despite his barbarism, despite his flaws. They are here for the totality of him, the summary of a man who is their friend.

Vraska hugs him close. He hates this. Anything he says carries weight, too much weight.

"I don't want you to go."

"I won't go far." He smiles.

He refuses to say goodbye. He knows she will, too. He reminds himself of something important, something he sears into his very self. _ I must ask for help. _

Jace feels tired. His coat is soaked with his own blood. He holds the knife in his chest like a talisman. Death is a change in state. Death is transformation. And courtesy of Vraska's final mercy, his passing is swift and just, a death he deserves.

"Thank you, Captain."

He loves her. She'll be all right. She's a survivor, too. He keeps his gaze on Vraska as his eyes blur, as his hand goes slack, as the world goes dark. He tries to say more but is too weak to speak. Instead, he feels death pull at him as a wave, a steady tug toward something quiet, something safe, something peaceful.

Jace slips away with Vraska's voice in his ear, echoing his own words back to him. "You are my curiosity. You are my empathy. You are every time I am unafraid to start over."

He has fixed his mistake, but he is no hero. He is only a man. He did it after all; he remembered who he was. His beloved kisses his forehead, and the last feeling in his heart is joy.

In the quiet of the sanctum with the now separated orrery overhead, Jace goes still.

Vraska holds him tight as he lets go.

And all along, Jace's clear-water eyes look directly into hers, unafraid.

